add('chn_2022_bed53a',5.5,'Taking reciprocals of successive increments reveals a geometric sequence and a simple ratio recurrence; the second part requires pairing terms in two reciprocal sums.',r'''Write $a=a_1$, $b=b_1$. For $d_n=a_n-a_{n+1}$, the recurrence gives $1/d_n=1/d_{n-1}+1/a_n$. Hence $d_n=a_nd_{n-1}/a_{n-1}$ and $a_{n+1}/a_n=a_n/a_{n-1}=a/(a+1)$. Similarly, with $e_n=b_{n+1}-b_n$, one has $b_n/e_n=b_{n-1}/e_{n-1}+2$, so $b_{n+1}/b_n=(b+2n)/(b+2n-1)$.

The first condition therefore gives $\frac a{a+1}\frac{b+200}{b+199}=1$, yielding $\boxed{a_1-b_1=199}$.

For the second part, monotonicity and $a_{100}=b_{99}$ give $a_i>b_i$ for $i\le99$. Comparing the reciprocal sums through index $99$ gives $a_{99}-a_{100}>b_{100}-b_{99}$, hence $a_{99}>b_{100}$. Now pair $a_i$ with $b_i$ for $i\le98$, $a_{99}$ with $b_{100}$, and $a_{100}$ with $b_{99}$. This proves $\sum_{i=1}^{100}1/a_i<\sum_{i=1}^{100}1/b_i$. Therefore $a_{100}-a_{101}>b_{101}-b_{100}$, so $\boxed{a_{100}+b_{100}>a_{101}+b_{101}}$.''',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Sequences & recurrences','Inequalities'],note='Corrected reciprocal-increment transcription signs and supplied the missing final comparison in part (2).')
