add('chn_2023_b3eef6',5.5,'A prime-power obstruction gives the exponent, while repeatedly merging the two smallest factors keeps every composite factor below the sharp threshold.',r'''The minimum is $\lambda=1/1012$. Indeed, for $n=p^{2024}$, any factorization into $2023$ positive integers has a composite factor at least $p^2=n^{1/1012}$, ruling out smaller exponents.

Conversely begin with all prime factors of $n$, counted with multiplicity. If there are at most $2023$, append ones. Otherwise repeatedly replace the two smallest current factors $u\le v$ by $uv$ until $2023$ factors remain. If there are $q\ge2024$ factors, their product satisfies $n\ge uv\,v^{q-2}\ge(uv)^{q/2}$, so the newly formed composite satisfies $uv\le n^{2/q}\le n^{1/1012}$. Thus every final factor is either an original prime or a composite no larger than the required bound. The case $n=1$ uses only ones.''',topic='Number Theory',subtopics=['Primes','Divisibility & GCD','Extremal arguments'])
add('chn_2023_364475',5.5,'Expands the kernel as prefix and suffix squares, applies the same adjacent-difference bound in both directions, and finds an alternating family approaching equality.',r'''Put $S_i=x_1+\cdots+x_i$, with $S_0=0$, and $T=S_n$. Expanding shows that the left side is
$$Q=T^2+\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}\bigl(S_i^2+(T-S_i)^2\bigr).$$
Using $(u-v)^2\le2u^2+2v^2$ on consecutive prefix sums gives $\sum x_i^2\le4\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}S_i^2+2T^2$. Applying the same argument to suffix sums gives $\sum x_i^2\le4\sum_{i=1}^{n-1}(T-S_i)^2+2T^2$. Average these inequalities to obtain $\sum x_i^2\le2Q$.

For sharpness take $x_1=1$, $x_i=2(-1)^{i-1}$ for $2\le i<n$, and $x_n=(-1)^{n-1}$. Then $T=0$, every other prefix sum is $1$ or $-1$, $Q=2(n-1)$ and $\sum x_i^2=4n-6$. The ratio tends to $1/2$ as $n$ grows. Hence $\boxed{C=1/2}$.''',topic='Algebra',subtopics=['Inequalities','Algebraic identities','Sequences & recurrences'])
