add('chn_2023_ca3b99',7,'Identifies a hidden antipodal circumcentre, transfers the midpoint by a power identity, and combines a homothety with isosceles-trapezoid relations.',r'''Let $O$ be the antipode of $A$ on the circumcircle of $ABC$. Since $OB\perp AB$ and $BP=BK$, the line $OB$ is the perpendicular bisector of $PK$; similarly $OC$ is the perpendicular bisector of $QK$. Thus $O$ is the circumcentre of $PQK$. If $X$ is the midpoint of $KT$, then $OX\perp AK$, so $X$ lies on the circle with diameter $AO$, namely the circumcircle of $ABC$. Power from $K$ gives $KA\cdot KX=KB\cdot KC$. If $M$ is the midpoint of $AK$, then $KM\cdot KT=KB\cdot KC$, proving $B,C,M,T$ cyclic.

Reflect $A$ in $B,C$ to obtain $A_1,A_2$. The isosceles trapezoids $KPA_1A$ and $KQA_2A$ give $AP=KA_1$, $AQ=KA_2$ and the corresponding angle equalities. The homothety of ratio $1/2$ about $A$ sends $A_1,K,A_2$ to $B,M,C$. Consequently the cyclicity just proved yields the stated angle relation.

For the length relation, triangle areas and the cyclicity give
$$\frac{BP}{CQ}=\frac{BK}{CK}=\frac{BT}{CT}\frac{\sin\angle BTK}{\sin\angle CTK}=\frac{BT}{CT}\frac{BM}{CM}=\frac{BT}{CT}\frac{KA_1}{KA_2}=\frac{BT}{CT}\frac{AP}{AQ}.$$
Rearrangement proves the desired product identity. Angles may be read as directed throughout when points lie on extensions.''',topic='Geometry',subtopics=['Triangle centres','Transformations','Power of a point & radical axes','Angles & cyclic quadrilaterals'])
